Ladybird exempts an extension page’s request to a URL the extension
has host access to from CORS: no preflight, no CORS check, and a basic
filtered response, while the request keeps CORS tainting, so it goes
out with the extension’s Origin and without cookies under the default
credentials mode; each redirect hop is judged again. Firefox reaches a
similar result through nsCORSListenerProxy::UpdateChannel(), which
skips CORS when the requesting principal’s CheckMayLoad() succeeds —
for an extension, through BasePrincipal::AddonAllowsLoad() — though
it then treats the request as same-origin, cookies included. Chromium
adds the extension’s host permissions to the network service’s
OriginAccessList (CreateCorsOriginAccessAllowList() in cors_util.cc),
and WebKit sets the extension’s pages’ _corsDisablingPatterns from
WebExtensionContext::corsDisablingPatterns(). Content scripts get no
exemption in any of them.
